    "summary": "Attention works like a mental spotlight, illuminating a narrow area of experience while the rest fades into the background. This metaphor, introduced by William James and refined by Michael Posner, captures how we select information from a vast sensory world.",
    "whyItMatters": "Understanding attention as a spotlight explains why we miss things outside our focus and why deliberate direction of that spotlight is the basis of concentration.",
    "takeaways": [
      "Attention highlights selected input and suppresses the rest",
      "The spotlight can be narrow or broad depending on the task",
      "William James and Michael Posner shaped the spotlight model"
    ],
    "keywords": ["attention spotlight", "selective attention", "Michael Posner", "focus"],
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "Can the attention spotlight be split between two things?",
        "answer": "Research suggests attention can sometimes split into two foci, but performance drops significantly. Most evidence supports a single movable spotlight that switches rapidly between locations rather than truly dividing."
      },
      {
        "question": "How does the spotlight model differ from other attention models?",
        "answer": "The spotlight model emphasizes spatial selection of information. Other models like the zoom lens model allow the spotlight to widen or narrow, while filter models focus on how information is selected by features like pitch or color rather than location."
      }
    ]
  },
  "cognitive-science--attention-and-focus--t-attention-as-a-limited-resource": {
